It returns `[null, {}]` — a 2-element array whose first element is `null` (the falsy result of `input.match(regex)`) and whose second is a fresh empty object.

```js
function match(input, regex, handlers) {
  const matches = input.match(regex);
  if (matches) { ...; return [matches, all]; }
  else { return [matches, {}]; }
}
```

On a match it returns `[matches, all]`: the raw regex match array plus an object mapping each non-literal handler's token first char to its deserialized value.

(Note: because the no-match branch returns `{}` rather than `null`, the caller's `matches ? dateTimeFromMatches(matches) : [null, null]` check is truthy, so it calls `dateTimeFromMatches({})` and gets `{}`.)

It returns a truthy `Invalid` object — specifically `unitOutOfRange("weekYear", obj.weekYear)`, i.e. `new Invalid("unit out of range", "you specified <value> (of type <typeof value>) as a weekYear, which is invalid")`. It never returns `false` and never throws in that case.

```js
export function hasInvalidWeekData(obj) {
  const validYear = isInteger(obj.weekYear), ...
  if (!validYear) {
    return unitOutOfRange("weekYear", obj.weekYear);
  } ...
}
```

(`isInteger` is `typeof o === "number" && o % 1 === 0`, so `undefined`, `null`, floats, strings, `NaN`, `Infinity` all fail it. Symbols/BigInts would throw a `TypeError` from `%`.)

It returns `LocalZone.instance` — the `LocalZone` singleton (`type === "local"`, valid), not `null`/`undefined`, and it does not throw.

```js
static get defaultZone() {
  return defaultZone || LocalZone.instance;
}
```

The module-level `defaultZone` starts as `null` (with the comment "not setting this directly to LocalZone.instance bc loading order issues"), and `normalizeZone` is only used by the `defaultZoneName` setter, not the getter.

It returns the string `"[error]"` — it does not rethrow and does not return `undefined`.

```js
run = function(code) {
  var result;
  try {
    result = eval(code);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
    return "[error]";
  }
  ...
}
```

The exception is caught, logged with `console.error(e)`, and the literal string `"[error]"` is returned.

After all the `example(...)` calls, the `examples` array is simply joined with an empty separator and inserted into the page HTML — it is not sorted, filtered, mutated, or returned.

```js
var all = "<h1>Some Luxon examples</h1>";
all += "<p>This is not meant to be a comprehensive showcase of Luxon's capabilities, just a quick flavoring.</p>";
all += "<table>";
all += examples.join("");
all += "</table>";

document.body.innerHTML = all;
```

Each `example(code)` call pushed one `<tr class='example'>...</tr>` string onto `examples`; `examples.join("")` concatenates them in insertion order between `<table>` and `</table>`, and `document.body.innerHTML = all` replaces the page body. The array itself is local and goes out of scope (the function returns `undefined`).

The default is `'latn'` — i.e. `/\d/` (ASCII digits 0–9).

```js
export function digitRegex({ numberingSystem }, append = "") {
  return new RegExp(`${numberingSystems[numberingSystem || "latn"]}${append}`);
}
```

There is no default parameter value; the fallback comes from `numberingSystem || "latn"`, so `undefined`, `null`, or `""` all yield `latn` (`numberingSystems.latn === "\\d"`). Note an unrecognized non-empty key yields `/undefined/` since there's no validation.

For any falsy value (`''`, `null`, `undefined`, `0`, `false`, `NaN`) it takes the same branch: `defaultZone = null` — `normalizeZone` is never called.

```js
static set defaultZoneName(z) {
  if (!z) {
    defaultZone = null;
  } else {
    defaultZone = normalizeZone(z);
  }
}
```

`defaultZone = null` is the module-level "unset" sentinel, so `Settings.defaultZone` then returns `defaultZone || LocalZone.instance` — the `LocalZone` singleton — and `Settings.defaultZoneName` returns that local zone's name (e.g. `America/New_York`), not `''`/`null`. (Note `0` would be a valid fixed-offset zone in `normalizeZone`, but the falsy check swallows it.)

It's an abstract stub: it unconditionally throws `ZoneIsAbstractError` (message `"Zone is an abstract class"`), rather than returning anything.

```js
/**
 * The type of zone
 * @abstract
 * @type {string}
 */
get type() {
  throw new ZoneIsAbstractError();
}
```

Every member of the base `Zone` class (`type`, `name`, `universal`, `isValid`, `offsetName`, `formatOffset`, `offset`, `equals`) is the same abstract stub; concrete subclasses like `FixedOffsetZone`, `IANAZone`, `InvalidZone`, and `SystemZone` override them.

It returns a `FixedOffsetZone` built directly from the numeric offset in minutes: `new FixedOffsetZone(matches.Z)` — not `FixedOffsetZone.instance(...)`, so no UTC singleton.

```js
let zone;
if (!isUndefined(matches.Z)) {
  zone = new FixedOffsetZone(matches.Z);
} else if (!isUndefined(matches.z)) {
  zone = IANAZone.create(matches.z);
} else {
  zone = null;
}
```

`matches.Z` comes from the `Z`/`ZZ`/`ZZZ` units via `signedOffset(h, m)` (offset in minutes, e.g. `+06:00` → `360`). The zone is the second element of the returned `[vals, zone]` tuple; `Z` is never copied into `vals` itself.
